<?php
/** Journal batch 6 (30 articles): new long-tail guides. Built with jm_blog_build(); see seed_blog_lib.php. */
function jm_blog_specs_6(): array {
    return [
    ['jawai-leopard-sighting-chances-how-likely', 'How Likely Are You to See a Leopard at Jawai?', 'Safari', 'jawai-leopard-watch', 'An honest look at sighting chances at Jawai, what improves your odds and how many drives to plan.', 'Jawai Leopard Sighting Chances: How Likely Are You to See One?', 'How likely is a leopard sighting at Jawai? Honest odds, the season and time of day that help, how many drives to plan and what to do if the cat stays hidden.', 'Jawai leopard sighting chances, will I see a leopard in Jawai, Jawai safari success, Jawai leopard probability, how many safaris Jawai', 'Jawai has a name for relaxed leopards resting on open rocks, but it is still wild country, and nobody can promise a sighting. What we can say is how to stack the odds in your favour.', [['Why Jawai is better than most places', 'Leopards here live on bare granite hills rather than in thick forest, so a resting cat can often be seen from the track with binoculars. Trackers who grew up in these villages know the favourite ledges, caves and water points, and read alarm calls from langurs and peafowl.'], ['What improves your chances', '- Go at first light or in the last two hours before sunset. || - Plan at least three drives over two days rather than a single one. || - Travel between October and June, when the hills are dry and open. || - Keep quiet, stay still and let the tracker set the pace.'], ['If the leopard stays hidden', 'Some drives end with only pugmarks, a fresh scrape or a distant call, and that is part of wildlife watching. The hills also give you hyenas, jackals, foxes, owls, eagles and a great deal of landscape, so a drive is rarely empty. A second or third drive is the best remedy.'], ['A realistic way to plan', 'Treat each drive as a fresh chance rather than a test. Guests who book three or four drives, stay patient and keep their expectations on the landscape as well as the cat usually leave happy. Early in the season, grass can still hide animals, while from February to June the open, dry hills make spotting easier. Weather matters too: cool, cloudy mornings sometimes keep leopards out longer.'], ['How trackers read the hills', 'Trackers study pugmarks on sandy tracks, scratch marks on trees, fresh kills and the alarm calls of langurs, peafowl and deer. They also know which ledges catch the first sun. Their knowledge of individual animals and territories is the real difference between a short and a successful drive.']], [['Is a leopard sighting guaranteed?', 'No. Jawai gives better chances than most places, but the animals are wild and free. We never promise a sighting.'], ['How many drives should I book?', 'Three or four drives across two days is a comfortable plan for most visitors.'], ['Does the season change my chances?', 'Yes. Dry months from March to June often give more open views, while winter gives the most comfortable weather.']], 'safari'],
    ['jawai-safari-age-limit-children-and-elders', 'Jawai Safari for Children and Older Guests: Comfort and Safety', 'Family', 'jawai-hills-view', 'What to know about age, comfort and pace before bringing children or older relatives on a Jawai safari.', 'Jawai Safari for Kids and Seniors: Age, Comfort & Safety', 'Planning a Jawai safari with children or older guests? Learn about comfort, pace, seating, seasonal timing and simple precautions for a calm private-jeep drive.', 'Jawai safari children, Jawai safari age limit, Jawai safari senior citizens, Jawai safari with family, private jeep Jawai comfort', 'A private open jeep makes Jawai one of the easier wildlife trips for mixed-age families, because the pace is yours to set. A few choices make it smoother still.', [['Children', 'Most children enjoy the drive if it is not too long, they have snacks and water and they are given a job such as counting birds. Winter dawn drives are cold, so warm layers and a cap matter more than anything. Younger children usually do better on the late-afternoon drive.'], ['Older guests', 'Tracks are bumpy in places, so mention knee, back or balance issues when you book. We can plan shorter drives, easier seating and more halts. Morning sun can be strong in summer, so carry a hat and water.'], ['Simple precautions', 'Everyone stays seated, nobody leans out and nobody gets down from the jeep except at the places your guide says are safe. Keep phones on silent and follow the tracker calmly.'], ['Choosing the right drive', 'For young children and older guests, a late-afternoon drive of about two to three hours is usually the easiest, with softer light and warmer temperatures. Morning drives are best for sightings but can be cold in winter. Tell us your preference and we will suggest the slot.'], ['Seating, health and medicines', 'Jeeps have bench seating with grab rails. Mention knee, back or heart conditions when you book, and carry any regular medicines and water. Bumpy sections are short, and the driver can slow down on request.']], [['Is there a minimum age for the safari?', 'We plan around comfort rather than a hard age rule. Tell us the ages when you book and we will suggest the right drive.'], ['Are the jeeps safe for older relatives?', 'Yes, with a steady driver and a gentle pace. Let us know about any health concerns so we can adjust the plan.'], ['Can babies come on the safari?', 'Very young children can come with their parents, but dust, cold and bumps make it a personal decision. Please talk to us first.']], 'safari'],
    ['jawai-hotels-near-leopard-hills-what-to-check', 'Choosing a Hotel Near the Jawai Leopard Hills: 10 Things to Check', 'Stay', 'hotel-front', 'A practical checklist for comparing hotels and stays around Bera and the Jawai leopard hills.', 'Hotel Near Jawai Leopard Hills: 10 Things to Check Before Booking', 'Comparing hotels near the Jawai leopard hills? Use this 10-point checklist: distance to the hills, safari arrangements, food, rooms, rates, access and guest support.', 'hotel near Jawai leopard hills, Jawai hotel booking tips, stay in Bera, Jawai hotel checklist, where to stay Jawai', 'Jawai offers a spread of stays, from village homestays to hotels and camps. The right one depends on what you value, and a short checklist saves a lot of regret.', [['Location and access', 'Ask how far the hotel is from the hills where drives start, whether the road is paved and how easy it is to reach after dark. Staying in Bera village keeps transfers short and early starts easier.'], ['Safari, food and rooms', '- Does the property run its own safaris with local trackers, or does it outsource them? || - Is the kitchen vegetarian, and can it handle special diets? || - Are rooms air-conditioned, with hot water and reliable power? || - Can the team be reached easily on WhatsApp before you arrive?'], ['Rates and policies', 'Rates often vary with the season, so ask what the quote includes and when it applies. Read the cancellation and change terms before you pay anything. Our own [rooms](rooms) start from ₹2,500 a night and are confirmed on WhatsApp.'], ['Questions to ask before you pay', 'Ask for the full list of what is included: breakfast, pickups, safari booking help, taxes and any seasonal surcharge. Ask what happens if a drive is cancelled for weather. Clear answers are a good sign that the property is run carefully.'], ['Reading reviews sensibly', 'Look for reviews that mention guides, rooms, food and responsiveness rather than only the view. Recent reviews matter more. One or two negative comments are normal, but a pattern is worth noting.']], [['Should I stay close to the hills?', 'Yes, if safaris are your priority. A shorter drive means more rest and a calmer start to the day.'], ['Do I need to book safaris separately?', 'Not at our hotel. We arrange private-jeep drives with local trackers for you.'], ['Is it better to book a package or separate items?', 'A package that includes stay, food and drives is simpler, but separate bookings give flexibility. Ask for both and compare.']], 'stay'],
    ['bera-to-jawai-dam-distance-and-how-to-visit', 'Bera to Jawai Dam: Distance, Best Time and How to Visit', 'Planning', 'jawai-bandh', 'How far the Jawai Dam is from Bera, the best hours to go and what to look out for around the water.', 'Bera to Jawai Dam: Distance, Timing & How to Visit', 'How far is Jawai Dam from Bera? Get the distance, drive time, best hours, seasons for birds and crocodiles, and simple rules for a safe, respectful visit.', 'Bera to Jawai Dam distance, Jawai Bandh visit, Jawai dam timing, Jawai dam birds, Jawai dam from Bera', 'The Jawai Dam, also called Jawai Bandh, is about 15 km from Bera, roughly half an hour by road. It makes a calm companion to a leopard drive.', [['Getting there', 'Most visitors go by private jeep or car, often as part of a wider drive through the hills. The road is simple, but livestock and village traffic call for patience. Ask the team for a pickup if you do not have a vehicle.'], ['When to go', 'Early morning gives soft light and active birds. In winter you may see flamingos, cranes and geese, while the monsoon fills the dam and turns the hills green. Midday can be harsh, especially in summer.'], ['Staying safe and respectful', 'Marsh crocodiles live here, so keep well back from the water and never walk toward the edge. Do not feed animals or leave litter, and keep voices low near roosting birds.'], ['What to bring', 'Binoculars, a hat, water and a light jacket in winter. A camera with a zoom lens helps for birds on the far shore. Wear closed shoes because the ground near the water can be muddy.'], ['Making a morning of it', 'A good plan is to leave Bera at first light, spend an hour at the water, then continue to a hill viewpoint and return for a late breakfast. Combine it with a village stop if the group is interested in local life.']], [['How far is Jawai Dam from Bera?', 'About 15 km, around 30 minutes by road.'], ['Can I combine the dam with a safari?', 'Yes. A birding and wetland drive is a popular half-morning plan.'], ['Is swimming allowed?', 'No. Crocodiles and deep water make it unsafe, so stay on the bank.']], 'plan'],
    ['jawai-leopard-caves-and-hill-shrines-what-to-know', 'Caves, Boulders and Hill Shrines: The Landscape Leopards Live In', 'Nature', 'jawai-granite-hill', 'How granite caves, boulder fields and small hill temples shape life for both leopards and herders at Jawai.', 'Jawai Granite Hills: Leopard Caves, Boulders & Hill Shrines', 'Learn how the granite caves, boulder fields and small hill shrines of Jawai shelter leopards and herders alike, and how to explore the landscape respectfully.', 'Jawai granite hills, leopard caves Jawai, Jawai hill temples, Jawai boulders, Jawai landscape', 'The first thing visitors notice at Jawai is the rock: domes, ledges and piles of giant boulders that look balanced by chance. That rock is the leopard’s home.', [['Why granite suits leopards', 'Crevices, caves and shaded ledges give shelter from heat and a clear view over the land. Leopards rest in the cool of a cave by day and move at dusk. Rainwater collects in rock pools that animals use in the dry months.'], ['Shrines among the rocks', 'Small shrines dot the hills, tended by local families. They are sacred places, so visit quietly, dress modestly and ask your guide before approaching. In many places people and leopards have shared the same slopes for generations.'], ['Exploring respectfully', 'Stay on the paths your guide chooses, never climb on boulders for photographs and leave nothing behind. Early and late light bring out the colour of the stone.'], ['Reading signs of life', 'Look for pale scat on rocks, scratches on tree bark, flattened grass and the neat tracks of a big cat on sandy ground. Your tracker will point them out and explain how old they look.'], ['Photographing the stone', 'Shoot at sunrise and sunset to catch the warm colours. A polarising filter helps with glare, and a wide lens captures the scale of the domes. Include a human figure only when it is safe and respectful.']], [['Can I walk in the hills?', 'Only with a guide and only where it is safe. Most visitors explore by jeep.'], ['Are the shrines open to visitors?', 'Some are, but always ask first and respect local custom.'], ['Are caves safe to enter?', 'No. Leopards use them, so never approach a cave. Watch from the jeep.']], 'safari'],
    ['why-the-jawai-hills-look-like-this-granite-geology', 'Why the Jawai Hills Look Like This: A Short Guide to the Granite', 'Nature', 'jawai-boulders', 'A plain-language explanation of the ancient granite domes and boulder piles around Bera.', 'Jawai Granite Geology Explained: Why the Hills Look This Way', 'A simple guide to the ancient granite of Jawai: how domes and balanced boulders form, why they suit leopards and the best light to photograph them.', 'Jawai geology, Jawai granite hills, Jawai boulders explained, why Jawai hills look like this, Jawai landscape photography', 'The Jawai hills are made of very old granite, shaped slowly by weather. Understanding how they form makes a drive more interesting and helps you read the land.', [['From rock to boulders', 'Granite cools underground and is later exposed. Water seeps into cracks, heats and cools with the seasons and gradually splits the rock into blocks, whose edges then round off. Over a very long time these become the domes and stacked boulders seen today.'], ['What it means for wildlife', 'Gaps between blocks become caves and dens, rain collects in hollows and the open slopes give predators and prey room to move. Grass, thorn scrub and a few hardy trees grow in the soil between the stones.'], ['Reading the landscape', 'Look for dark streaks of water stain, pale lichen on stone and flat sun-warmed slabs where animals rest. In the golden hour the rock glows orange and pink, which is why photographers love it.'], ['Weathering and water', 'Where water collects, small pools form on the rock. They fill in the monsoon and can last into winter, drawing birds and animals. Streaks and stains on the stone show where water runs.'], ['A landscape still changing', 'The process continues today, slowly. Frost, heat, roots and rain keep loosening blocks. What looks fixed is actually a patient, long-term process.']], [['How old are the Jawai hills?', 'They are among the oldest rock landscapes in India, formed over a vast span of time.'], ['When is the stone most photogenic?', 'At sunrise and sunset, when low light warms the granite.'], ['Can I climb the boulders?', 'Not without a guide. They can be slippery and leopards may rest nearby.']], 'safari'],
    ['what-to-wear-on-jawai-safari-by-season', 'What to Wear on a Jawai Safari, Season by Season', 'Planning', 'jawai-leopard-rest', 'Winter layers, summer cotton and monsoon cover: dress right for every season in an open jeep.', 'What to Wear on a Jawai Safari: Winter, Summer & Monsoon', 'Dress for comfort on a Jawai safari: warm layers for winter dawns, light cotton and sun protection for summer and rain cover for the monsoon, in muted colours.', 'what to wear Jawai safari, Jawai safari clothing, winter safari clothes India, summer safari clothes Rajasthan, Jawai packing', 'You sit in an open jeep for about three hours, so what you wear matters. The rule everywhere is muted colours, layers and sensible shoes.', [['October to February', 'Mornings can be close to freezing in December and January. Wear a base layer, a fleece or sweater, a windproof jacket, a scarf and gloves. Take them off as the sun rises.'], ['March to June', 'Light cotton full sleeves, a wide-brimmed hat, sunglasses and a scarf for dust. Carry water and sunscreen, and drink often.'], ['July to September', 'A light rain jacket and quick-dry clothes, plus a waterproof cover for your camera. Closed shoes with grip help on wet ground.'], ['Layering that works', 'Start with a thin base layer, add a warm mid layer and finish with a windproof outer layer. This lets you adjust as the sun rises. A scarf doubles as a dust cover and neck warmer.'], ['Small things that help', 'Carry a cap or hat, sunglasses, a water bottle and lip balm. Gloves are welcome on cold mornings, and a thin blanket is a luxury on the winter drive.'], ['Fabrics and fit', 'Cotton and light wool are comfortable and quiet. Avoid rustling nylon and shiny buckles. Loose trousers are easier in a jeep than tight jeans.']], [['Which colours are best?', 'Olive, khaki, brown and grey. Avoid bright colours and strong perfume.'], ['Do I need special shoes?', 'Closed, comfortable shoes are enough. Hiking boots are not required.'], ['Do I need a jacket in summer?', 'A light jacket helps in the early morning, even in summer.'], ['Is white clothing a problem?', 'White stands out. Neutral earth tones are better.']], 'plan'],
    ['one-day-jawai-photography-plan-sunrise-to-sunset', 'A One-Day Jawai Photography Plan: Sunrise to Sunset', 'Photography', 'jawai-leopard-sunset', 'A realistic shot list and timetable for photographers with a single day at Jawai.', 'One-Day Jawai Photography Plan: Sunrise to Sunset Shot List', 'Plan a one-day photography trip to Jawai with a simple timetable, shot list for leopards, birds, boulders and village life, plus gear, light and etiquette tips.', 'Jawai photography plan, one day Jawai, Jawai photo tour, Jawai shot list, leopard photography Jawai', 'One day is enough to come away with strong images if you plan around the light. Here is a timetable that works.', [['Dawn drive', 'Be at the first good ledge as the sun rises. Look for a leopard resting on warm rock, long shadows and mist over the dam. Keep the lens ready and the engine switched off when the guide asks.'], ['Midday', 'Rest, back up your cards and clean your gear. If you must shoot, look for textures, birds in shade and village life, and avoid hard overhead light on faces and fur.'], ['Evening drive', 'Return about two hours before sunset for warm light on boulders and silhouettes of trees or herders. Bring a spare battery, since cold drains them quickly.'], ['Settings that work', 'Use aperture priority, a fast shutter (at least 1/1000 s for movement) and continuous autofocus. Shoot in RAW and bracket exposure when the sun is behind the subject.'], ['After the shoot', 'Back up your files the same night. Review images, cull the weaker ones and plan what you want to improve on the next drive.'], ['Looking after your gear', 'Dust is the enemy. Keep lenses capped between shots, change lenses in the hotel, and use a blower rather than a cloth. Cold mornings and warm rooms can cause condensation, so let your camera adjust slowly.']], [['Which lens should I carry?', 'A 200–500 mm zoom for animals and a wide zoom for landscapes. A beanbag steadies the lens on the jeep.'], ['Is flash allowed?', 'No. Flash disturbs animals and is not used on our drives.'], ['Can I use a drone?', 'No. Drones disturb wildlife and are not allowed on our drives.'], ['How many batteries should I bring?', 'Two or three for a full day, since cold reduces battery life.']], 'safari'],
    ['what-to-order-at-a-vegetarian-restaurant-in-bera', 'What to Order at a Pure Vegetarian Restaurant in Bera', 'Food', 'food-thali-rajasthan', 'A beginner’s guide to ordering Rajasthani village food when you are hungry after a safari.', 'What to Order at a Vegetarian Restaurant in Bera, Jawai', 'Hungry after a Jawai safari? Learn what to order at a pure vegetarian restaurant in Bera: dal baati churma, ker sangri, gatte, bajra roti, kadhi, chai and sweets.', 'vegetarian restaurant Bera, what to eat Jawai, Rajasthani food Bera, dal baati churma Bera, pure veg restaurant Jawai', 'Rajasthani village food is hearty, vegetarian by tradition and made for working people. It is also very good after a cold morning drive.', [['The classics', 'Start with dal baati churma, then try ker sangri and gatte ki sabzi. Bajra roti with ghee and garlic chutney is a village staple, and kadhi with rice is a gentle, tangy comfort dish.'], ['Light options', 'If you prefer lighter food, ask for a plain dal, seasonal vegetables with phulka and a bowl of curd. Our kitchen can reduce oil and spice on request.'], ['Sweets and chai', 'Finish with moong dal halwa or malpua when available, and a kulhad of masala chai. See the [restaurant](restaurant) page for the current menu.'], ['Ordering for a group', 'Order a mix of one dry dish, two gravies, a lentil dish and bread or rice. Share everything and add chutneys and papad. Ask the team what is freshest that day.'], ['Dietary needs', 'Tell the kitchen about Jain, allergy or no-onion-no-garlic needs. Plain dal, rice and roti are always possible.'], ['Eating after a safari', 'After a cold dawn drive, a hot bowl of dal, bajra roti with ghee and masala chai is perfect. Order breakfast the evening before if you leave very early, so the kitchen has it ready.']], [['Is the restaurant fully vegetarian?', 'Yes. Our kitchen is 100% vegetarian.'], ['Can you make less spicy food?', 'Of course. Tell the team and they will adjust the spice.'], ['Is there a children’s option?', 'Yes. Plain dal-rice, khichdi and roti are easy choices.'], ['Is the food oily?', 'Rajasthani food is rich, but the kitchen can go light on ghee and oil if you ask.']], 'food'],
    ['kadhi-pakoda-and-rajasthani-curries-explained', 'Kadhi, Gatte and Other Rajasthani Curries Explained', 'Food', 'food-gatte', 'The tangy, gram-flour and yoghurt curries that define Rajasthani home cooking.', 'Rajasthani Curries Explained: Kadhi, Gatte & Ker Sangri', 'A guide to Rajasthani vegetarian curries: kadhi pakoda, gatte ki sabzi, ker sangri and panchmel dal, with what they taste like and how to enjoy them.', 'Rajasthani curries, kadhi pakoda, gatte ki sabzi, ker sangri, Rajasthani vegetarian food', 'In a dry land with few fresh greens, cooks built curries from yoghurt, gram flour, lentils and dried beans. The results are tangy, rich and deeply satisfying.', [['Kadhi and gatte', 'Kadhi is a yoghurt and gram-flour curry, often with soft pakoras. Gatte ki sabzi uses steamed gram-flour dumplings in a spiced gravy. Both are eaten with rice or hot roti.'], ['Ker sangri and panchmel dal', 'Ker sangri combines wild desert berries and long beans, tempered with spices. Panchmel dal blends five lentils for a rounded, earthy flavour, traditionally with baati and churma.'], ['How to enjoy them', 'Eat them hot with ghee, a little pickle and fresh onion. Share several dishes, so you can taste the range.'], ['The role of yoghurt and spice', 'Yoghurt gives body and tang, gram flour thickens, and tempering of cumin, mustard seed and red chilli finishes it. The balance changes from home to home.'], ['Pairing and serving', 'Serve kadhi hot with plain rice and a dry vegetable. Gatte pairs with bajra roti. Ker sangri sits well with phulka and a little ghee.'], ['Where the dishes come from', 'Many came from the cooking of desert communities who stored dried ingredients for the long dry season, as well as from royal kitchens that refined them. That is why simple ingredients taste so layered.']], [['Are these dishes spicy?', 'They can be, but they can also be mild. Ask for your preferred level.'], ['What goes best with them?', 'Bajra roti, plain rice, phulka or baati.'], ['Are they heavy?', 'They are rich, so share dishes and add a light salad.'], ['Can I ask for a vegan option?', 'Many dishes use ghee or yoghurt, but plain dal, bajra roti with chutney and some vegetable dishes can be made vegan. Ask the team.']], 'food'],
    ['bajra-roti-and-millets-of-rajasthan', 'Bajra Roti and the Millets of Rajasthan', 'Food', 'food-bajra-dal', 'Why pearl millet is the grain of the desert, and how it is cooked and eaten around Jawai.', 'Bajra Roti & Rajasthan Millets: The Grain of the Desert', 'Why pearl millet (bajra) is Rajasthan’s staple grain: how bajra roti is made, what to eat it with, its nutrition and how to try it near Jawai.', 'bajra roti, Rajasthan millets, pearl millet Rajasthan, bajra recipe, Rajasthani village food', 'Wheat is a recent comfort in many Rajasthani villages. For generations, the daily bread was bajra, a hardy millet that grows where little else will.', [['A grain built for dry land', 'Pearl millet tolerates heat and poor soil and needs little water, so it suits this region well. It is rich in fibre and keeps you warm in winter, which is why it is a cold-season favourite.'], ['How it is eaten', 'Dough is patted by hand into thick rotis and cooked on a griddle or open flame. They are served with ghee, jaggery, garlic chutney, buttermilk or a simple vegetable.'], ['Try it at Jawai', 'Ask the kitchen for bajra roti with ghee and chutney. It pairs well with dal and seasonal vegetables.'], ['Other millets', 'Jowar (sorghum) and ragi (finger millet) are also eaten, though bajra is the regional favourite. Each has a different taste and texture.'], ['Cooking tips', 'Use warm water, knead to a soft dough and pat gently so the roti does not crack. Cook on a hot griddle and finish on the flame.'], ['Health and tradition', 'Millets are rich in fibre and minerals, and traditional diets paired them with ghee, buttermilk and greens. Today they are popular again with people looking for gluten-free and slow-digesting grains.']], [['Is bajra roti gluten-free?', 'Pearl millet is naturally gluten-free, but check with the kitchen about cross-contact if it matters.'], ['When is it best?', 'In winter, fresh and hot.'], ['Can I make bajra roti at home?', 'Yes, with practice. Ask the kitchen team for tips.'], ['Is bajra suitable for diabetics?', 'It is a good option in moderation, but ask your doctor for personal advice.']], 'food'],
    ['rajasthani-pickles-chutneys-and-papad-guide', 'Rajasthani Pickles, Chutneys and Papad: A Taster’s Guide', 'Food', 'food-thali-rajasthan', 'The small dishes on the side of the plate that make Rajasthani meals memorable.', 'Rajasthani Pickles, Chutneys & Papad: A Taster’s Guide', 'Discover the pickles, chutneys and papad served with Rajasthani meals: garlic chutney, mirchi achar, kair sangri pickle, mint chutney and roasted papad.', 'Rajasthani pickles, garlic chutney, mirchi achar, papad Rajasthan, Rajasthani thali sides', 'The main dishes get the attention, but the little bowls on the side do a lot of work. Here is what you will meet on a Rajasthani thali.', [['Chutneys', 'Red garlic chutney, ground with chillies, is the classic partner for bajra roti. Mint and coriander chutneys cool the plate, while sweet tamarind chutney goes with kachori and snacks.'], ['Pickles', 'Mirchi achar, lemon pickle and a seasonal mixed pickle add sourness and heat. Ker sangri also appears as a pickle in some homes.'], ['Papad and crisps', 'Roasted or fried papad, made from lentil dough, adds crunch to a plate. Ask for it roasted for a lighter option.'], ['Balancing a plate', 'A little pickle goes a long way. Take a small taste with each bite of roti, and use chutney to cool or heat the plate as you like.'], ['Regional variations', 'Every home has its recipe. Garlic chutney is red and strong in some villages, milder in others. Ask your host about local differences.'], ['Taking flavour home', 'Local shops sell packed pickles, chutney powders and papad. Choose sealed jars, check the date and ask whether airline rules allow liquids in hand baggage. Wrap glass jars well.']], [['Are the chutneys very hot?', 'Garlic and chilli chutneys can be fiery. Try a small taste first.'], ['Can I buy pickles to take home?', 'Local shops sell them. Ask the team for trusted places.'], ['Which is the mildest chutney?', 'Mint and coriander chutneys are usually the gentlest.'], ['Are pickles very oily?', 'Many use mustard oil generously, which also preserves them. Ask for a small portion first.']], 'food'],
    ['jawai-honeymoon-itinerary-2-nights-3-days', 'Jawai Honeymoon Itinerary: Two Nights, Three Days', 'Couples', 'jawai-leopard-sunset', 'A slow, private plan for couples: leopard drives, quiet evenings and good food.', 'Jawai Honeymoon Itinerary: 2 Nights 3 Days for Couples', 'A relaxed 2-night, 3-day Jawai honeymoon plan: private jeep drives at sunrise and sunset, village walk, stargazing and Rajasthani dinners in Bera.', 'Jawai honeymoon itinerary, Jawai couples trip, Jawai 2 nights 3 days, romantic Jawai, honeymoon Rajasthan', 'Jawai suits couples who like space, silence and landscape. This plan keeps mornings gentle and evenings unhurried.', [['Day 1', 'Arrive by afternoon, settle in and take a late-afternoon private jeep drive with sunset on the granite. Dinner is Rajasthani and vegetarian.'], ['Day 2', 'Dawn drive for leopards, a long breakfast, rest, then a quiet village walk or a visit to the dam. After dark, step outside for stargazing.'], ['Day 3', 'A last sunrise drive or a slow morning, then check out. If you are travelling on, Ranakpur and Udaipur are natural next stops.'], ['Romantic touches', 'Request a quiet table, a cake or flowers when you book. A sunset drive with a flask of tea is simple and memorable.'], ['If you have an extra day', 'Add a visit to Ranakpur or a slow day at the hotel. See [day trips from Jawai](blog/day-trips-from-jawai-ranakpur-kumbhalgarh-mount-abu).'], ['Packing for two', 'Carry warm layers in winter, comfortable shoes for the rocks and a small flask for evening tea. A camera or phone for sunsets is all you need. Leave room for local crafts.']], [['Can we arrange a special dinner?', 'Tell us the occasion when you book and we will see what we can arrange.'], ['Is private time possible?', 'Yes. Every drive is in a private jeep.'], ['What is the best season for couples?', 'November to February for weather, March to June for sightings.'], ['Can we plan a private dinner?', 'Tell us the date and occasion and we will see what is possible.']], 'safari'],
    ['jawai-family-itinerary-3-days-with-children', 'Jawai Family Itinerary: Three Days with Children', 'Itinerary', 'jawai-rabari', 'A gentle three-day plan that mixes wildlife, village life and plenty of rest for families.', 'Jawai Family Itinerary: 3 Days with Children', 'A child-friendly 3-day Jawai plan: short private-jeep drives, a village visit, dam and bird watching, easy meals and rest time between activities.', 'Jawai family itinerary, Jawai with kids 3 days, family trip Rajasthan, Jawai children activities, Bera family stay', 'Children enjoy Jawai most when the days are not packed. This plan alternates short activities with rest.', [['Day 1', 'Arrive, rest and take a short late-afternoon drive to see the hills and look for peacocks. Early dinner and early night.'], ['Day 2', 'A morning drive, long breakfast and relaxed afternoon with games and a village walk. Visit the dam if the children are keen on birds.'], ['Day 3', 'One last short drive, then breakfast and departure. Carry snacks, water and a small bag of things to do in the car.'], ['Keeping children engaged', 'Hand over a checklist of animals, a small notebook and child-size binoculars. Let them choose the next stop. Keep snacks and water within reach.'], ['Rest is part of the plan', 'Allow a quiet hour after lunch. Tired children make difficult passengers, so shorter drives and gentler afternoons pay off.'], ['A sample daily rhythm', 'Morning drive, long breakfast, rest, light lunch, a short activity, early evening snack and early dinner. Keeping to a routine suits small children, and the quiet of Bera makes it easy.']], [['Are the drives long for children?', 'Drives last about three hours, but can be shortened. Tell us when you book.'], ['What should we pack for kids?', 'Warm layers in winter, caps, sunscreen, snacks, water and a small binoculars.'], ['Are there playgrounds?', 'No, but the hotel has open space and the village offers simple walks.'], ['Are there baby-friendly meals?', 'Yes. Plain dal, khichdi, rice, curd and soft rotis are always possible.']], 'safari'],
    ['jawai-friends-group-trip-ideas', 'Jawai with Friends: Group Trip Ideas and Planning Tips', 'Itinerary', 'jawai-village-celebration', 'How to plan a trip for a group of friends: drives, rooms, meals and a few evenings to remember.', 'Jawai with Friends: Group Trip Ideas & Planning Tips', 'Planning a Jawai trip for a group of friends? See how to split jeeps, share rooms, plan meals and fill evenings, with ideas for a relaxed weekend.', 'Jawai group trip, Jawai friends weekend, Jawai trip planning, group safari Rajasthan, Bera stay group', 'A group of friends can have a great time at Jawai if the plan has some structure and some freedom.', [['Jeeps and timing', 'A private jeep carries up to about six guests, so larger groups travel in several jeeps. Book them for the same slot so you can compare sightings afterwards.'], ['Rooms and meals', 'Share rooms to keep costs down and arrange group meals at the restaurant. Tell us about dietary needs in advance.'], ['Evenings', 'Plan a campfire, stargazing, a short village walk or simply a long dinner. Ask the team for ideas that suit your group.'], ['Splitting costs', 'Agree early on who pays for what: rooms, jeeps, meals and transport. A shared kitty makes the trip less stressful.'], ['Keeping everyone happy', 'Include one early drive, one easy day and one shared activity. Respect those who want to sleep in or photograph more.'], ['Evening ideas', 'Ask for a campfire, music on a small speaker at a respectful volume, card games or a late-night tea. Keep noise down after dark so the village and animals stay undisturbed.']], [['How many people can we book for?', 'Tell us your group size on WhatsApp and we will check availability.'], ['Can we plan a celebration?', 'Yes, tell us the occasion and the number of guests.'], ['Can we get a group meal?', 'Yes. Tell the team the number and any dietary needs.'], ['Is alcohol available?', 'Our kitchen and restaurant are vegetarian. Please ask the team about house rules.']], 'plan'],
    ['how-to-reach-ranakpur-from-bera-route-and-timing', 'How to Reach Ranakpur from Bera: Route, Timing and Tips', 'Day trips', 'place-ranakpur', 'The drive to the Ranakpur Jain temples, how long it takes and how to fit it with a safari.', 'Bera to Ranakpur: Route, Distance, Timing & Tips', 'How to reach Ranakpur from Bera: distance of about 48 km, drive time, best hours for the Jain temples, dress code and how to combine it with a Jawai safari.', 'Bera to Ranakpur, Ranakpur from Jawai, Ranakpur temple timings, Ranakpur day trip, Jawai Ranakpur route', 'Ranakpur is around 48 km from Bera, about an hour by road, and is the easiest heritage trip from Jawai.', [['The drive', 'The road runs through villages and then into the forested Aravalli foothills. Hire a car or ask your hotel to arrange one.'], ['At the temples', 'The complex is known for hundreds of carved marble pillars. Dress modestly, remove footwear and check timings for non-Jain visitors, as they differ.'], ['Fitting it into a safari trip', 'Do a dawn safari, have breakfast and leave by 9:30 AM. You can be back by afternoon. Combine it with Kumbhalgarh if you start early.'], ['Best hours', 'The temples are quietest in the morning and again in late afternoon. Midday can be hot, and tour buses arrive around noon.'], ['Eating on the way', 'There are simple vegetarian places near the complex. Carry water, and keep small change for donations and footwear storage.'], ['Dress and respect', 'Cover shoulders and knees, and be ready to leave leather items and some accessories at the entrance. Switch off phones in prayer areas and follow staff guidance.']], [['How far is Ranakpur from Bera?', 'About 48 km, roughly one hour.'], ['Can I visit Ranakpur and Kumbhalgarh in a day?', 'It is possible with an early start, but a relaxed pace is better.'], ['Is photography allowed inside?', 'Rules vary. Ask at the entrance before using a camera.'], ['How long should I allow at Ranakpur?', 'Two hours is enough for the main temple